      ))}
    </ol>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>What Happens at Each Stage, in More Detail</h2>
    <p>
      The six steps above cover the outline; here's what's actually going on
      at the stages people usually have questions about.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Booking and feasibility.</strong> When you send an item list on
      WhatsApp, the team is checking three things: whether your area is
      serviceable, whether the item mix is something a standard pickup can
      handle (versus needing a scheduled bulk or ITAD-style visit), and
      whether anything in the list — a swollen battery, a server rack, a
      pile of CRT monitors — needs advance planning. This is also when any
      quote discussion starts, since resale or scrap value depends on
      condition and quantity, not just item type.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Collection and initial sorting.</strong> At pickup, items are
      grouped by broad category — working electronics, non-working
      electronics, batteries, and data-bearing devices — rather than by
      brand or exact model. Batteries are physically separated from the
      rest of the load at this point specifically because they're handled
      through a different downstream process than general electronics; see
      <a href="/blog/why-electronics-should-not-go-in-household-waste/">why
      electronics shouldn't go in household waste</a> for the safety reasoning.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Data destruction.</strong> Anything with storage — laptops,
      desktops, phones, external drives, servers — is set aside for data
      handling before it moves further into the process. Depending on the
      device and what's agreed at booking, that can mean a software wipe, a
      degaussing pass for magnetic media, or physical shredding for drives
      that are damaged or where the customer wants destruction rather than
      wiping. See <a href="/data-destruction/">data destruction</a> for how
      the different methods compare.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Assessment for reuse.</strong> Working devices don't go
      straight to material recovery. They're checked for functional
      condition and, where there's genuine resale or reuse value, routed
      toward that instead — see <a href="/sell-electronics/">sell
      electronics</a> for how condition-based quotes work. This step is why
      recycling and reuse aren't really competing options; reuse is simply
      the first check inside the recycling process itself.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Material recovery and responsible disposal.</strong>
      Whatever's left after reuse assessment and data handling — devices
      that are broken beyond practical repair, or too old to have resale
      demand — goes to material recovery. Metals like copper, aluminium and
      steel are extracted for reuse in manufacturing; components that
      contain hazardous material are routed to handling processes designed
      for them rather than general disposal.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Where the Materials Actually Go</h2>
    <p>
      It's a fair question: once a device is taken apart, what actually
      happens to the pieces? In general terms, disassembled electronics
      separate into a handful of broad material streams:
    </p>
    <ul class="check-list">
      <li><strong>Metals</strong> — copper wiring, aluminium casings, steel frames and small amounts of other recoverable metals go back into manufacturing supply chains rather than being newly mined.</li>
      <li><strong>Plastics</strong> — casings and housings are sorted by plastic type where practical, since mixed plastics are harder to process than a single grade.</li>
      <li><strong>Batteries</strong> — handled through a separate stream entirely because of fire risk and the different chemistries involved (lithium-ion, lead-acid, nickel-based) — see <a href="/battery-recycling/">battery recycling</a>.</li>
      <li><strong>Glass and displays</strong> — screens and CRT glass require careful handling distinct from general glass recycling.</li>
      <li><strong>Hazardous components</strong> — anything containing materials like lead solder or certain flame retardants is routed to handling designed specifically for those materials, not general waste streams.</li>
    </ul>
    <p class="muted-note">
      Exactly which downstream partner handles which material stream, and
      what recovery rates look like for a specific device, varies by item
      and isn't something we'll put an exact number on here without a real
      source to cite — if that level of detail matters for your situation
      (a compliance audit, for instance), ask directly and we'll point you
      to what documentation is available.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>E-Waste Recycling for Households vs Businesses</h2>
    <p>
      The basic recycling process is the same regardless of who's booking,
      but the practical shape of a household pickup and a business pickup
      tends to look quite different.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Households</strong> typically bring a mixed, irregular batch —
      a few old phones, a laptop that's been in a drawer for years, some
      cables and a dead router, maybe an inverter battery. These pickups are
      usually one-off or occasional, don't need documentation beyond a
      confirmation, and the main preparation is backing up and wiping
      personal devices — see
      <a href="/blog/how-to-prepare-electronics-for-recycling/">how to
      prepare electronics before recycling</a>.
    </p>
    <p>
      <strong>Businesses and offices</strong> more often deal with larger,
      more uniform batches — a fleet of laptops being refreshed, retired
      servers, decommissioned networking equipment — where documentation,
      asset tracking and certified data destruction matter for internal or
      regulatory records. These jobs typically route through our
      <a href="/itad/">ITAD (IT asset disposition)</a> service rather than a
      standard household pickup, since the process includes asset lists and
      agreed documentation rather than a simple item count.
    </p>
    <p>
      If you're not sure which category your situation falls into — say, a
      small business with a modest number of devices — just describe what
      you have on WhatsApp and the team will point you to the right process
      rather than making you guess.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>E-Waste Rules and Your Responsibility as a Consumer</h2>
    <p>
      E-waste in India is governed separately from general municipal waste,
      under the E-Waste (Management) Rules framework, which puts recovery
      and recycling obligations on producers (a system called Extended
      Producer Responsibility, or EPR) while also shaping how collection and
      recycling is expected to work downstream. Two things worth knowing as
      a consumer, not a producer:
    </p>
    <ul class="check-list">
      <li>You're not expected to know the regulatory detail — that's the recycler's obligation to operate compliantly, not yours to verify line by line.</li>
      <li>Choosing a pickup over a general bin or an unverified informal buyer is the practical way an individual consumer participates in that system.</li>
    </ul>
    <p>
      If you want the fuller picture — what EPR actually means, or what the
      current rules cover — we've written these up separately rather than
      compressing them here: see
      <a href="/blog/what-is-epr-in-e-waste/">what is EPR in e-waste
      management</a> and
      <a href="/blog/e-waste-management-rules-2022/">E-Waste Management
      Rules 2022, explained simply</a>. For documentation questions specific
      to your own pickup, see <a href="/trust/">Trust &amp; Compliance</a>.
    </p>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>Common Myths About E-Waste Recycling, Corrected</h2>
    <div class="myth-grid">
      <div class="myth-card">
        <p class="myth-label">Myth</p>
        <p class="myth-text">"Deleting files or doing a factory reset is enough before I recycle a device."</p>
        <p class="fact-label">Fact</p>
        <p>A factory reset removes the everyday view of your data, not the underlying data itself — recoverable with the right tools unless the storage is properly wiped or physically destroyed. See <a href="/data-destruction/">data destruction</a> for what actually clears it.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="myth-card">
        <p class="myth-label">Myth</p>
        <p class="myth-text">"A broken device has nothing worth recycling."</p>
        <p class="fact-label">Fact</p>
        <p>Non-working devices still contain recoverable metals and, in some cases, working components. A device not powering on doesn't disqualify it from pickup — mention the condition and it'll be handled appropriately.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="myth-card">
        <p class="myth-label">Myth</p>
        <p class="myth-text">"An informal scrap buyer is basically the same as a recycler, just cheaper."</p>
        <p class="fact-label">Fact</p>
        <p>The difference isn't price — it's accountability. An informal buyer generally can't confirm how data is handled, how batteries are processed, or where materials end up. A verified recycler can answer all three.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="myth-card">
        <p class="myth-label">Myth</p>
        <p class="myth-text">"Batteries can just go in with the rest of the electronics."</p>
        <p class="fact-label">Fact</p>
        <p>Batteries are collected in the same visit but processed through a separate stream, because different battery chemistries carry real fire and chemical-handling risks that general electronics don't.</p>
      </div>
      <div class="myth-card">
        <p class="myth-label">Myth</p>
        <p class="myth-text">"Recycling e-waste always costs money."</p>
        <p class="fact-label">Fact</p>
        <p>Standard household and office pickups are free; some items may even carry resale or scrap value depending on condition and market demand. Unusual jobs (very large volumes, special handling) are discussed upfront, not charged as a surprise.</p>
      </div>
    </div>
  </section>

  <section>
    <h2>A Note on Safety Before You Book</h2>
    <p>
      Two things are worth flagging clearly rather than leaving implicit.
      First, if any battery in your pickup is swollen, leaking, or visibly
      damaged, say so when you book and keep it away from heat and away from
      stacking other items on top of it in the meantime — this is a genuine
      fire-risk item, not routine e-waste. Second, if a device holds
      personal or business data you haven't backed up yet, do that before
      pickup day if at all possible; once a device leaves for data
      destruction, recovery isn't the goal and shouldn't be relied on.
      Neither of these should stop you from booking — they're just worth
      doing in the right order.
    </p>
  </section>

